# PowerShell files MUST check out with CRLF on Windows. signtool appends its signature block
# assuming CRLF; on an LF-only .ps1 it silently eats the final line, producing a file that
# reports "Status: Valid" yet fails to parse with MissingEndCurlyBrace. Learned the hard way.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.psd1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1  text eol=crlf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Everything under host/ is destined for the Linux scanner, including files that are not .sh.
# gmp.env.example matters most: the operator copies it to .gmp.env, and the relay SOURCES that with
# /bin/sh as root. Measured on the target (Ubuntu, /bin/sh -> dash): sourcing a CRLF file gives a value
# with a trailing carriage return -- GMP_PASS='abc' yields length 4, not 3 -- so a Windows checkout of
# this template would produce credentials that fail to authenticate while looking correct in an editor.
# Quoting does not save it; dash keeps the CR either way.
host/*  text eol=lf

*.md    text
